  "body": "Fixes #257.\n\n`binaryMultiMMCPredictionEstimate` (`cpp/non_iid/multi_mmc_test.h`) initialises its 16 prefix dictionaries with a loop that reads `S[d+1]` for `d = 0..15`, guarded only by `assert(L > 3)`. For binary inputs of 4 ≤ L ≤ 16 that reads past the sample buffer. AddressSanitizer at `87c104d`:\n\n```\nERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow ... READ of size 1\n    #0 binaryMultiMMCPredictionEstimate(...) multi_mmc_test.h:38\n    #1 multi_mmc_test(...) multi_mmc_test.h:154\n    #2 main non_iid_main.cpp:453\n0x... is located 0 bytes after 8-byte region     (L = 8)\n```\n\nThe non-binary path guards the identical loop with `d < N`. This change adds `d+1 < L` to the binary loop's condition and nothing else.\n\n**Verification (Apple clang 21, macOS, asserts enabled as in the shipped Makefile):**\n- ASan build of this branch: L = 4, 8, 16 no longer report any sanitizer error (they still abort afterwards in `binaryLZ78YPredictionEstimate`'s `assert(L > B_len)`, reported separately in #262).\n- For L ≥ 17 the new bound is never reached. `-vv` output of this branch on all 11 files under `bin/` is byte-identical to the unpatched `87c104d` build.\n\nOne note for the issue text: on a normal (non-sanitizer) build the over-read is silent inside MultiMMC, but the run then aborts in the LZ78Y assert (exit 134) rather than printing an assessment; I have added that correction as a comment on #257.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
